WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:06.360
He was beloved by many as Theo Sipowicz on&nbsp;
NYPD Blue, but Austin Majors' tragic death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:12.420
at age 27 devastated his friends, family, and&nbsp;
the millions who grew up watching him on TV.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:18.180
On February 13, 2023, TMZ reported that&nbsp;
actor Austin Majors died at the age of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.440
27. According to the publication, he&nbsp;
passed away on Sunday, February 12,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.400
while staying at a homeless living facility&nbsp;
located in Los Angeles, California.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.520
An unspecified source told TMZ that Majors' death&nbsp;
may have come as a result of him ingesting a fatal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:39.060
amount of fentanyl, but this is unconfirmed as&nbsp;
of February 14, 2023. The autopsy is set to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:42.840
completed soon, with the results said&nbsp;
to arrive sometime within the next few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:47.520
months. Coinciding with the news of his death,&nbsp;
Majors' family released a statement that read,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.400
"[He] was a loving, artistic, brilliant, and kind&nbsp;
human being. Austin took great joy and pride in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.800
his acting career. He was an active Eagle Scout&nbsp;
and graduated Salutatorian in High School. He went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.440
on to graduate from USC's School of Cinematic Arts&nbsp;
with a passion of directing and music producing."

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:09.540
They also added that the entire family will carry&nbsp;
fond memories of their time with him, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:14.220
they will miss him forever. Though his acting&nbsp;
career wasn't long, Austin Majors racked up some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:19.260
high-profile credits and entertained audiences&nbsp;
the world over before his untimely passing.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:24.060
He got his start in the entertainment world as&nbsp;
a child actor, starting out strong with a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:29.460
feature-length credits. Among his earliest film&nbsp;
appearances were Nevada and The Prince of Air from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:35.820
1997 and 2000, respectively. In 2002, he even got&nbsp;
the chance to try out voice acting in the Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:41.220
flop, Treasure Planet, where he took on the role&nbsp;
of a young Jim Hawkins. While he'd continue to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:45.240
appear at the movies intermittently throughout&nbsp;
the remainder of his career, there's no denying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.280
that Majors' television work is what put him on&nbsp;
the map. Most famously, Majors appeared quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:57.240
frequently on the police procedural NYPD Blue,&nbsp;
popping up in 48 episodes between 1999 and 2004.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:02.700
"Theo, Theo, don't, don't plop your mashed&nbsp;
potatoes all over the table. C'mon. Just eat up."

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:06.960
His work in the role of Theo Sipowicz,&nbsp;
the son of Detective Andy Sipowicz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.100
paved the way for him to try out&nbsp;
other TV ventures, including ER,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:16.680
NCIS, and Desperate Housewives. However, none&nbsp;
of these programs kept Majors around nearly as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:22.080
long as NYPD Blue ultimately did. His final&nbsp;
acting credit is a Season 4 episode of How I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:27.780
Met Your Mother titled "Right Place Right Time,"&nbsp;
rounding out a pretty solid yet brief filmography.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.680
Following his time as an actor, Austin&nbsp;
Majors decided to chart a new course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.880
in life. As mentioned previously, he made his&nbsp;
way to the University of Southern California,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:40.860
where he took up a major in film and television&nbsp;
production with a minor in music on the side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:46.200
Instead of using his education to become a movie&nbsp;
star or return to being a frequent face on TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.400
Majors chose to continue pursuing different&nbsp;
passions in the field of entertainment.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.200
Majors maintained a clear interest in working&nbsp;
in film and television — as evidenced by his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.400
LinkedIn page, where he expressed his desire&nbsp;
to do everything from directing to editing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.800
in various kinds of visual media. Furthermore,&nbsp;
Majors would also embrace his musical side. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.360
began producing music under the name "Pope !",&nbsp;
and even had some songs available on Spotify.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.820
It was clear that no matter the avenue, Austin&nbsp;
Majors had an insatiable desire to follow and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:20.520
succeed at his creative passions. By family,&nbsp;
friends, and fans alike, he will be sorely missed.

